WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.513
(playful, upbeat music)

00:00:01.513 --> 00:00:02.424
- [Voiceover] Money.

00:00:02.424 --> 00:00:03.757
It's everywhere.

00:00:06.335 --> 00:00:07.439
But what happens when your bills get old

00:00:07.439 --> 00:00:09.509
and need to be replaced by new ones?

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:12.093
That means old cash needs to be destroyed.

00:00:12.093 --> 00:00:13.260
A lot of cash.

00:00:14.209 --> 00:00:16.898
This is not, however, the story
of money growing on trees,

00:00:16.898 --> 00:00:19.756
but of trees, growing out of money.

00:00:19.756 --> 00:00:21.260
Well, more like vegetables.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.239
(bright music)

00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:25.350
- We are here at the Federal
Reserve Bank in New Orleans,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:29.267
and this is where we
shred millions of dollars.

00:00:30.828 --> 00:00:33.465
My name is Dean Wortha, I'm
the lead business analyst

00:00:33.465 --> 00:00:36.021
for cash services here
at the New Orleans Fed.

00:00:36.021 --> 00:00:37.789
We are the nation's bank.

00:00:37.789 --> 00:00:41.001
Our job is to make sure that
we have a supply of currency.

00:00:41.001 --> 00:00:43.953
Also part of that is to
ensure that we remove unfit,

00:00:43.953 --> 00:00:46.650
old dirty currency from circulation.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.757
So here on average, we shred
about six million dollars

00:00:49.757 --> 00:00:52.657
in dirty money every day.

00:00:52.657 --> 00:00:54.562
This could be for any number of reasons.

00:00:54.562 --> 00:00:56.945
It could be because the
note has rips in it,

00:00:56.945 --> 00:00:59.244
holes in it, tears in it.

00:00:59.244 --> 00:01:00.624
It could have tape on it,

00:01:00.624 --> 00:01:02.767
it could have graffiti
written all over it.

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:05.611
We don't want that note
going back into circulation.

00:01:05.611 --> 00:01:07.819
If it has any sort of those qualities,

00:01:07.819 --> 00:01:09.786
it will be shredded.

00:01:09.786 --> 00:01:12.254
What we used to do is take
all of these currency shreds

00:01:12.254 --> 00:01:16.421
and they would just wind up
as waste, going to a landfill.

00:01:18.137 --> 00:01:19.977
Now, through a lot of work and effort,

00:01:19.977 --> 00:01:22.540
we finally figured out a way
where we can take these shreds

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.873
and ultimately recycle them.

00:01:25.771 --> 00:01:27.802
- [Voiceover] So all
that green leaves the Fed

00:01:27.802 --> 00:01:29.600
and heads to a compost facility

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.639
where it turns into
something quite useful.

00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:35.929
- What you're looking at right
now is 24 million dollars.

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:39.778
But the soil that is
creates will be priceless.

00:01:39.778 --> 00:01:41.489
My name is Johnathan Christian.

00:01:41.489 --> 00:01:43.406
We turn cash into soil.

00:01:45.102 --> 00:01:48.184
It is definitely one of
our secret ingredients.

00:01:48.184 --> 00:01:52.351
It's little in content, but
it's huge in what it provides.

00:01:53.631 --> 00:01:56.196
Currency that's deemed unfit
is brought to our facility

00:01:56.196 --> 00:01:57.392
at Wooden Materials.

00:01:57.392 --> 00:02:00.721
From there we go through
a simple composting process

00:02:00.721 --> 00:02:04.221
where we convert that into a healthy soil.

00:02:05.121 --> 00:02:07.998
Our compost is used
primarily by urban farmers

00:02:07.998 --> 00:02:10.410
in the greater New Orleans area.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.802
- [Voiceover] One of these
farmers is Simond Menasche.

00:02:12.802 --> 00:02:15.802
- And this is a million dollar farm.

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:20.986
We are making fresh, local
food accessible in New Orleans.

00:02:23.512 --> 00:02:27.679
We've grown veggies, herbs,
cucumbers, tomatoes and peppers.

00:02:29.795 --> 00:02:34.648
The vegetables we grow here are
made out of composted cash.

00:02:34.648 --> 00:02:38.324
It is very fulfilling to be growing,

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:42.925
using a material that would
otherwise go to waste.

00:02:42.925 --> 00:02:47.199
One man's trash is another man's treasure.

00:02:47.199 --> 00:02:48.032
- [Voiceover] And that's the story

00:02:48.032 --> 00:02:51.032
of how we grow tomatoes out of cash.

